uses the new Variant Transforms

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.840
tool for breakthrough clinical
data science with BigQuery.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.300
This is interesting from a
genomics perspective for those

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.980
out there who are in that space.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.600
So Color is a health
services company

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.180
that offers affordable and
accessible genetic testing,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.120
and they wanted to be
able to do more heavy data

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.700
mining on the data that they
have to better understand

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.230
their client DNA.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.980
So variance is a popular
task in genomic analysis,

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.740
and it's basically identifying
a variant of a specific genome

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.746
from some reference genome.

00:03:32.746 --> 00:03:34.370
And so BigQuery was
something that they

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.470
went with because
apparently it's

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.027
very mature in
terms of its ability

00:03:38.027 --> 00:03:39.860
to handle the type of
data sets that they're

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.710
working with because it's
fully managed data warehouse

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.640
and it has strong genomic
data import mechanism.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.460
And the Variant Transforms
is a recently released tool

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.980
that's basically-- and
it's an open source tool.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.212
It's out on GitHub.

00:03:53.212 --> 00:03:54.290
And we'll have a link.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.450
But it's basically
able to handle

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.060
massive amounts of genomic
data directly and load it

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.320
into BigQuery for you.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.199
And it works with Data Store,
too, as far as I understand.

00:04:03.199 --> 00:04:05.240
But yeah, we'll post that
out there for you guys.

