00:05:09.849 --> 00:05:12.452
All right, well why don't we
go chat with Dr. Fei-Fei Li?

00:05:12.452 --> 00:05:13.910
MELANIE WARRICK:
So today's podcast

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.190
we're excited to have join
us Dr. Fei-Fei Li, who

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.390
is our chief scientist of
AI and ML for Google Cloud,

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.752
and she is the
Associate Professor

00:05:23.752 --> 00:05:25.710
in the computer science
department at Stanford,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.360
who's currently on sabbatical.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:27.974
So welcome.

00:05:27.974 --> 00:05:28.890
FEI-FEI LI: Thank you.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.219
MELANIE WARRICK: And you
know, before we get started,

00:05:31.219 --> 00:05:32.760
we know about your
background, but we

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.170
would like to have you tell
in your own words who you are

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:37.530
and what you do.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:42.060
FEI-FEI LI: I guess I consider
myself an AI technologist

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.480
and researcher foremost.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.240
I've been a professor for most
of my professional career.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.640
And before taking
the sabbatical,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.970
I've been Director
of Stanford AI lab.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:58.150
And here at Google, I'm Chief
Scientist of AI and machine

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:03.690
learning, overseeing bringing
AI technology to the enterprise

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.320
world through Google Cloud.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.570
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.130
And in terms of doing
the work that you're

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.213
doing at Google Cloud,
what are some of the things

00:06:10.213 --> 00:06:12.150
that as a chief
scientist do you do?

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.980
FEI-FEI LI: It's an
incredibly fascinating world.

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:18.720
Here, you know, Cloud is the
biggest computing platform

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.280
that humanity has ever invented
to deliver computing service

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.110
to people and businesses.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.570
So on this platform, our
goal is to really democratize

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:33.490
AI services and products
to as many businesses

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.170
and as many people as possible.

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:40.380
And so Google Cloud offers
a suite of AI products,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:46.980
from APIs to customized models
to more close collaboration

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.400
and partnership
services to really try

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.080
to enable businesses to
solve their critical problem

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.220
through AI solutions.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.540
We also in the
meantime advance AI

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.830
technology, because as
you get close to solving

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:08.370
real-world problems,
new challenges arise,

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.670
and that inspires the
development of new AI

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:12.960
technology.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.160
And we work closely with
Google's AI research

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.850
and development teams
throughout the entire company

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.574
to advance AI as well.

00:07:22.574 --> 00:07:23.490
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.940
And I know that there was
recent release of, like, AutoML,

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.740
and there's been
the advancements

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.780
with TPUs and TensorFlow.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.470
What are some of the things that
you've seen, especially from,

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:36.630
like, applied AI, that you
think are very relevant in terms

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.030
of what's important for
businesses to be aware of?

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.980
FEI-FEI LI: Yeah, so
that's a great question.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.800
I want to first take
AutoML as an example

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.595
because that's a product that
really, truly excites me.

00:07:47.595 --> 00:07:51.370
It bears the meaning and
mission of democratizing AI

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:52.770
beautifully.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.710
We see that more
and more business

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.880
would like to use
AI as a service

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.370
to improve their products.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:08.610
And much of the power comes into
making predictions and doing

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:12.270
smart analytics of the
data the business have.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.090
But it used to be,
you know, still

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:19.020
is true that AI is a
pretty technical field.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:22.500
It's also young enough
that not every business

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:28.140
has the kind of AI talent
that Stanford or Google has.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.330
So the barrier of
entry is pretty high.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.880
So we've been thinking about how
to lower the barrier of entry

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.309
for businesses to use AI.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:40.539
So let's take image
as an example.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:42.990
If you are a real
estate company,

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:47.130
maybe you want to tag different
kind of houses or rooms.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.220
If you are a retail
clothing company,

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.620
you want to take an
image and tag shoes

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.170
or different kind of
shoes or clothings.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:59.460
If you are a nonprofit
tracking wild animals,

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.430
you would like to
take photos and tag

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.890
leopards versus giraffe.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:08.720
So there's a lot of need
for this kind of AI work.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.470
Well, one way to
solve the problem

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.480
is through what we call APIs.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.890
These are pre-trained model
that Google engineers have

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:21.990
built that work fantastically,
and companies can just

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:27.630
upload their images and get,
say, a label from the pictures

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.030
using these API models.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.970
But there's one thing that
API doesn't do for you,

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.920
which is customization.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.630
The API results come
from a pre-trained model

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:42.480
that has a pre-designed
set of labels.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.910
It's a lot of labels,
thousands of labels,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.860
but it's still pre-designed.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:51.060
But if you truly cares about
just tracking wild animals

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.440
or cares about
satellite imagery,

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:58.530
you're now in the realm of
a need for customization.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:02.040
And in fact, lots and lots
of businesses and people

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.430
need customization and they
don't have the capacity

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:09.480
to do it from data curation all
the way to creating the model.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.450
So we saw that as a
important opportunity

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:16.410
and created this new
product called AutoML,

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:20.370
starting from image
understanding or image tagging.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:24.060
That it's really
almost a plug-and-play

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:29.490
that customers can give us their
data that they want to label,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:35.760
and we built a specific
customized model for them

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.110
to use.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:38.860
I'm not going to get
into the details,

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.050
but the model is built on some
of the cutting-edge Google AI

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:43.550
technology.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:45.799
MELANIE WARRICK: This must
be really exciting for you,

00:10:45.799 --> 00:10:48.510
especially considering I know
you started out researching

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.880
around computer
vision, and to see

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.510
where it has gone from when you
started to where we are now,

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:56.070
and how it's being
more democratized,

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:58.140
as it's being more accessible.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:02.010
And I know you are one of the
inventors of ImageNet, which

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.620
is this well-known
competition that

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.070
was really pivotal in the
advancement of computer

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.316
vision, which came
to a close last year.

00:11:11.316 --> 00:11:12.690
And one of the
questions I wanted

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.231
to ask you is just
sort of a sideline

00:11:14.231 --> 00:11:16.050
is, how do you feel
seeing something

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:19.140
that you built that helped
advance a space that

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.320
matters so much to you, how do
you feel now that it's closed?

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:23.820
What does that mean to you?

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.880
FEI-FEI LI: So first of all,
I think ImageNet is generally

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:27.910
a group effort.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.270
So many of my
colleagues, students

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.870
are part of the core
creation team of ImageNet.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.690
And as a team, we
are very proud.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:40.520
We're proud that ImageNet has
made a contribution, computer

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:41.540
vision.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:44.160
It is one of the
driving forces of what

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.830
we see as the deep
learning revolution.

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:51.510
And closing down the competition
in the academic world

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.480
is really just the
beginning of a new chapter.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.290
First of all, Kaggle is
now hosting ImageNet.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.140
So the democratization
of ImageNet data

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:04.290
and this competition
itself lives on,

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.410
and it's reaching
to more developers.

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:11.430
It's also a sign
of the progress.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.130
Scientific discovery
and innovation

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:19.680
is always built upon the
continuous renewal of ideas.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.080
And ImageNet served a
important historical purpose.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:28.680
When we decided to finish
the academic competition,

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:33.460
we recognized the field is
ready to embrace new ideas.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:36.600
So that's where we are
with computer vision

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:37.560
and machine learning.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.180
MARK MANDEL: For those
people who aren't as familiar

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.040
with, say, ImageNet, can
you explain a little bit

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.810
about what that is,
and also the transition

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:43.630
that it's gone through?

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.088
You said it's gone
into Kaggle now.

00:12:45.088 --> 00:12:47.970
FEI-FEI LI: So ImageNet
was a project my students,

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:52.410
collaborator, and I
did starting 2007.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:57.240
The end product by 2009 is
a gigantic image data set

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:02.460
of 15 million images labeled
over 22,000 categories

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:05.940
of objects and real
world objects and things

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:10.140
and concepts, and publicly
released to the world of AI

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.270
research and education.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:17.040
It was-- the goal of making
ImageNet was to reboot machine

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:20.520
learning research and
rethink the relationship

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:23.160
between algorithm and data.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:26.160
It was built at
the time that data

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:32.490
was not as recognized the
key ingredient of AI systems.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.010
ImageNet's contribution,
I think part of it

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:41.170
is because of that it enabled
the family of algorithms

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:45.330
called neural network
to show its potential.

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:48.900
And because of that
effort, we think

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:51.960
it was important to encourage
the academic and research

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:56.310
community to benchmark their
algorithms on ImageNet.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:01.860
So we created the ImageNet
competition started in 2009.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:03.960
Actually, starting in 2010.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.020
And that competition
served as a good platform

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.380
for international
teams to benchmark

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:13.500
the progress we're making
in computer vision.

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:17.650
And the transition in
2017 is that we recognize,

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:20.890
just as I said before,
we've come a long way.

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:23.250
The problem that
ImageNet set out

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:26.010
to do-- object,
classification, and detection--

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.290
is I wouldn't call
largely solved,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.810
but is made so much
progress that we

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:34.455
need to create a space for
new problems and new ideas.

00:14:34.455 --> 00:14:36.979
MELANIE WARRICK: To inspire
new things, new ways

00:14:36.979 --> 00:14:39.270
to solve these types of
problems versus solve different

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:39.870
problems.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:40.703
FEI-FEI LI: Exactly.

00:14:40.703 --> 00:14:43.500
That's why we transitioned it
from a academic competition

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.492
to a Kaggle data set.

00:14:45.492 --> 00:14:46.950
MELANIE WARRICK:
So in terms of AI,

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.890
I've seen you talk about
how you like to emphasize

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.590
that it's not artificial.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:53.940
Why the emphasis that
it's not artificial?

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.907
And is this related to,
also, in reaction to AI hype?

00:14:56.907 --> 00:14:59.490
Do you have any thoughts around
that that you'd want to share?

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:00.156
FEI-FEI LI: Yes.

00:15:00.156 --> 00:15:04.890
I now tend to say that I share
with my students and colleagues

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.730
that the field of AI is
nothing but artificial.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:13.560
And what I really mean is that
this is a deeply, deeply human

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:17.620
pursuit, because
AI as a technology

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:20.400
is going to influence and
impact our human lives

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:23.290
and human society in
very profound ways.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:27.910
And as technologists, I
think it's really important

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.890
to recognize that.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:34.160
Humanity has never created
a technology that resemble

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:38.110
ourselves as much as AI does.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.900
And of course, part
of ourselves is

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.420
being able to function with
the level of logical reasoning

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:45.440
and computation.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:48.490
But part of being human
is also recognizing

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:53.240
the social relationships, the
emotion, the wanting to do good

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:54.190
and all that.

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:59.500
And since AI as a technology
is so powerful and impacting

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.530
so many different
sectors of the world,

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:05.560
we want to recognize
its human impact,

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:09.757
and also prepare ourselves
to AI's human impact.

00:16:09.757 --> 00:16:11.590
MELANIE WARRICK: So off
of that human impact

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:13.715
and also off of what you
were saying about ImageNet

00:16:13.715 --> 00:16:16.420
and trying to look into the
next phase of what research

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:18.100
would look like,
I know you've also

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:20.380
talked about the
new goalposts being

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:22.510
around natural
conversation, collaboration,

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:24.032
emotional perception.

00:16:24.032 --> 00:16:26.240
What do you think in terms
of where things are going?

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:28.150
And what types of
benchmarks are needed

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:29.962
for those types of goals?

00:16:29.962 --> 00:16:31.670
FEI-FEI LI: Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:37.600
I think that if you look at the
complexity and the intricacy

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:42.670
of human intelligence, being
able to classify or group

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:44.840
concepts is only one of them.

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:47.810
That's what mostly
what ImageNet did.

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:51.940
But whether it's our visual
ability or our navigation

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.850
ability or our natural
language communication

00:16:54.850 --> 00:17:00.010
ability or comprehension,
reasoning, emotion,

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.540
humans are really complex.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:06.849
So I think in order to think
about the next step of AI

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:10.859
it's really important to
go beyond the simple tasks

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:13.750
that we are starting
to excel at,

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:18.310
but to recognize the
complexity of intelligence.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:21.430
There are already some
interesting budding research.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:24.369
Google and many other
groups are looking deeply

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:27.670
at things like, for example,
natural language dialogue.

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:30.370
For me that's a very
interesting area

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:33.370
where you go beyond just
labeling parts of sentences

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:37.630
to actually enabling a
machine-human dialogue

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:39.790
in meaningful ways.

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:43.290
For vision, starting to
tell the story of a picture,

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:45.400
starting to reason
on the picture,

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:49.780
starting to understand the
events, the human activities,

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:54.021
the object functions, and all
those would be the next step.

00:17:54.021 --> 00:17:56.020
MELANIE WARRICK: And take
what we've excelled at

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:57.936
and do what you've been
talking about in terms

00:17:57.936 --> 00:18:00.430
of democratizing it and making
it accessible for people

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:02.140
who use it.

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:04.024
So your research,
one of the areas

00:18:04.024 --> 00:18:05.440
that you've been
focused on lately

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:06.610
is more in the health sector.

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:07.750
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:08.770
about what are
some of the things

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.540
that you're exploring
in that space?

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:11.290
FEI-FEI LI: Right.

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:14.320
So you're referring to
some of my collaborations

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:17.920
with Stanford School of Medicine
and also Stanford Children's

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:20.720
Hospital and adult hospital.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:25.495
So a few years ago we thought
deeply about AI transformation,

00:18:25.495 --> 00:18:28.660
and one of the most
exciting transformation

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:31.540
that everybody hears about
is self-driving cars.

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:35.950
And this is the
result of advances

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:39.400
from sensing technology
to perception technology

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:42.220
to machine learning
technology and all that.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:44.980
And then we recognize
that there is actually

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:48.450
a parallel opportunity
in health care

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:51.070
that has more analogy
to self-driving car

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:52.810
than most people recognize.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:56.410
It's really about
the space of care,

00:18:56.410 --> 00:19:01.330
is the how do we enable care
in a more effective way.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:05.290
For example, in hospitals there
is a lot of things going on,

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.780
and work could be
more efficient.

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:10.180
Errors should be avoided.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:13.750
Patients can be
monitored further.

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:19.810
Or in elder homes, can we
enable more intelligent ambient

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:22.720
sensing and machine
learning to help

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:27.400
families and clinicians to
take care of our elders,

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:28.820
and all that.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:32.500
And we start to recognize, wow,
this is the kind of technology

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:35.540
that's needed in these
potential services,

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:37.500
is very similar to
self-driving cars.

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.020
It uses smart sensors
to gather information

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:42.550
from the environment.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:46.060
And then back end, it uses
computer vision and machine

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:49.630
learning techniques to
recognize activities

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:51.640
and to make decisions.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:55.840
So we started to collaborate
with Stanford Hospital and also

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.560
a senior home facility
in San Francisco

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:05.620
to explore how we can use AI to
help avoid health care workflow

00:20:05.620 --> 00:20:10.150
errors or to help aging seniors,
especially early detection

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:12.100
of dementia and all that.

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:15.160
And a specific
example is a product

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:18.250
we're doing with Lucile
Packard Hospital at Stanford

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:22.510
where we put smart sensors or
depth sensors in the hospital

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:27.790
unit to help doctors and nurses
monitor their hand hygiene

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:28.990
practice.

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:31.300
It turned out the
lack of hand hygiene

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:34.030
or hospital-acquired
infection is

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:38.170
one of the leading killers
of our patients in hospitals.

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.980
It kills more people than
car accidents per year.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:45.790
And how do we continuously
monitor the behavior

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:49.600
of proper hand hygiene
practice is actually

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:52.180
a very difficult task.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:56.670
And it's expensive to deploy
people to watch people.

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.660
And that is very sparse.

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:01.210
It introduces bias.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:04.480
So we thought, wow, the
same kind of depth sensor

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.300
self-driving car uses
to watch the pedestrians

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.660
and cars on the road
could be actually put

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:12.220
into the hospitals.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.260
So we installed
these smart sensors

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:16.810
that are also
privacy preserving,

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:20.230
and use computer vision
technology in the back end

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:24.190
to be able to continuously
monitor and observe

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:27.940
hand hygiene practice
in the hospital unit.

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:32.350
And that was one of the first
kind ever done in that way.

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:36.100
And we're very excited to see
some initial results showing

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:42.880
that a automatic
observing system using AI

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:47.560
does as good, even better
actually than human observers,

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:49.040
but it doesn't get tired.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.729
It's not biased and all that.

00:21:50.729 --> 00:21:52.270
MELANIE WARRICK:
That would be great.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:54.550
And this was a recent
implementation,

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:56.400
so the results haven't
come back just yet.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:22:00.810
FEI-FEI LI: We actually, it's
in submission to a publication.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:01.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Got it.

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:02.600
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:03.100
Nice.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:05.140
I'd love to take a slightly
different tack here.

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:06.899
This work, it sounds fantastic.

00:22:06.899 --> 00:22:09.190
But you've been in the AI
and machine learning industry

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:11.060
for a really long time and
you've watched it grow,

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:13.143
and you were talking a bit
about barriers to entry

00:22:13.143 --> 00:22:15.160
and, like, the speed at
which it's progressing.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:17.935
If people are coming in new to
machine learning and AI now,

00:22:17.935 --> 00:22:19.817
and they're interested
in getting involved,

00:22:19.817 --> 00:22:21.400
where should they
go, what should they

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.880
do, what path do you
think they should take?

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.050
FEI-FEI LI: Yeah, good question.

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.030
I get that a lot as a professor.

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:32.680
So I think it depends on
what you're interested in.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:37.010
If you are interested in
machine learning as a research,

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:40.360
then I think it's important to
get involved in the research

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:43.690
projects that, I assume
that's when you have already

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:45.880
some technical background.

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:49.720
If you're interested in the
machine learning implementation

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:51.910
and software engineering,
there is already

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:53.800
a lot of online courses.

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:56.860
And you know,
Google's TensorFlow

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:59.020
and the TensorFlow
community has a lot

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:02.500
of materials that
can help to get

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.140
your hands on to the
coding and software

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.000
engineering of the site.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:10.990
If you're interested in using
machine learning as a way

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:16.030
to create interesting startups,
entrepreneurial opportunities,

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:19.750
start thinking about the pain
points of different businesses

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:24.820
and products, and find
areas where using smart data

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.790
analytics or machine
learning techniques can help

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:29.800
solve a pain point.

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:32.590
But machine learning and
AI is not just technical.

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:34.720
That goes back to
my original point.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:39.430
There is actually a lot of
related areas, machine learning

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:41.110
and policy, right?

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:45.340
Or AI and education,
AI for sustainability,

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.920
AI for economics.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:51.490
There's a lot of
now new areas that

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:55.360
are at the cross-section of AI
technology and human impact.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:58.930
So a lot of people, even
without technical background,

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:00.645
can get involved and contribute.

00:24:00.645 --> 00:24:02.020
MELANIE WARRICK:
You know, I know

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:04.925
that you get asked
a lot about the AI,

00:24:04.925 --> 00:24:06.550
sort of what are the
bigger challenges.

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:09.100
And you spearhead efforts
around diversity inclusion,

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:11.980
and you have this wonderful
organization, AI For All.

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:14.944
Can you tell us a little bit
about that and what it does?

00:24:14.944 --> 00:24:15.610
FEI-FEI LI: Yes.

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:21.970
So AI is a field that largely
stemmed from computer science.

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:25.210
And I think it's
pretty obvious by now

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:28.900
that the general field of
computer science and AI

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.990
has a critical issue
of lack of diversity.

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:33.410
We lack women.

00:24:33.410 --> 00:24:36.800
We lack underrepresented
minority.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.930
All the publicly released data,
whether it's Silicon Valley

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:46.120
companies or academia, we're
seeing a very small percentage

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.880
of women, and even
smaller percentage

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:52.010
of underrepresented
minority in this field.

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:55.810
And I think that is a
pity, because involving

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:59.350
more diversity would
make our field better.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.850
And we talk about
several reasons.

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:03.910
One is for economic
and labor reason.

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:07.180
We need more and
more CS AI talents,

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:10.100
and we need to tap
into all walks of life.

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:12.820
It's also an innovation
creativity reason

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:15.590
that when diverse group
of people coming together,

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:19.290
solutions and ideas
are more innovative.

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:22.170
And it's also a fairness
and justice reason.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:26.210
When we want a technology
to resemble or to represent

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:28.820
the values of our
collective society,

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:32.180
we want all walks of
life to be involved

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:34.600
in developing and creating it.

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:38.840
For these three reasons, at
Stanford about four years ago,

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:42.860
we started this program to
encourage diversity group

00:25:42.860 --> 00:25:45.830
of high school
students to participate

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:50.180
in the study and early
hands-on research of AI.

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:54.800
And we created this program
called Stanford AI Lab Outreach

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:55.880
Summer.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:59.990
And at Stanford we were
focusing on high school girls

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:02.810
around 10th grade,
and we bring them

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:06.020
on campus for two to
three weeks in the summer,

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:07.625
and they study AI.

00:26:07.625 --> 00:26:11.600
But also, they study AI
and its human mission

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:14.030
and human-centered impact.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:16.850
And that has been very,
luckily, successful.

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:19.950
So we decided in
2017 to expand it

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:22.750
to a national
nonprofit organization.

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:27.140
We call it AI For All
to really replicate

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:32.480
the Stanford model and create
summer camps nationwide.

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:35.630
And so we started with
Berkeley last year,

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:38.990
where they reached out
to low-income families

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:40.770
and students.

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:42.590
And I'm focusing on robotics.

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:45.710
2017 we're expanding
to Princeton

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:50.450
to underrepresented racial
minority students to study AI

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:51.870
and policy.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:54.560
We are expanding
to CMU this year

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:57.830
as well to reach out
to rural population.

00:26:57.830 --> 00:27:01.516
And so we're expanding to
six different sites 2018,

00:27:01.516 --> 00:27:03.214
and we'll continue this effort.

00:27:03.214 --> 00:27:05.630
MELANIE WARRICK: And we plan
